Q: SQL query running slow A: <p>T N's answer covers off most of where your issue will be. When it comes to querying in EF, do as much client-side as practical before filtering, so for instance when checking for a null/empty, do that in your controller/service before applying a <code>Where()</code> clause. You should also do things like length assertions. (I.e. avoid users searching on &quot;a&quot;, etc) which would be extremely inefficient.</p>
<p>Another client-side check where if you want a single search term field (rather than separate entries for name, email address, &amp; phone number) is to inspect the search term to determine if it matches the likeness of an email address, phone number, etc. before applying it.  Pass the term to a method to inspect if it looks like an email address, and if so, filter on email, if not, pass to a method to inspect (and format) it for a phone number, if that doesn't match, filter on name.  For a simple one-term search text, aim for the most likely, and efficient searches. If the user doesn't find what they are looking for, then give them the option for a more specific search. (I.e. fields or a selection for what they specifically want to search across)</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(term) || term.Trim().Length &lt; 4)
    throw new ArgumentException(&quot;Search term is too short.&quot;); // Should be validated client-side so this method isn't called with invalid terms.

var emailResult = isEmailAddress(term);
var phoneResult = isPhoneNumber(term);

if (emailResult.IsMatch)
    query = query.Where(x =&gt; x.Email == emailResult.Value);
else if (phoneResult.IsMatch)
    query = query.Where(x =&gt; x.PhoneNumber == phoneResult.Value);
else
    query = query.Where(x =&gt; x.Name.StartsWith(term));

</code></pre>
<p>Where the isEmailAddress/isPhoneNumber methods return a Tuple or simple result class with a Boolean for whether they match or not, and the formatted term to use in the query when matched.</p>
<p>This can help keep the SQL as compact as possible, and running efficiently for the majority of search cases. If you need to support a more <code>Contains()</code> type of search, provide that as a more advanced search option that users can choose, and specify which field they want to search across rather than trying to make all searches as flexible as possible. Doing so makes 90% or more of the searches far slower than they need to be, just to cater for the edge cases.</p>

Q: Download a CSV file using PHP A: <p>You can use below code to save file in server </p>

<pre><code>fopen("filename.csv","w")
</code></pre>

<p>Then you can write code to download saved file by two ways</p>

<p><strong>First</strong></p>

<p>You can read file and pass to browser with headers</p>

<pre><code>header('Content-type: text/csv');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="filename.csv"');
ob_clean();
readfile($filePath);
exit;
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Second</strong></p>

<p>You can redirect to file path like</p>

<pre><code>header("Location: ".SITE_URL."VALID_PATH/filename.csv"); 
exit;
</code></pre>

Q: PowerMockito Mocked final class gson.fromJson() Returns null A: <p>If you were to use the standard <code>@Mock</code> and <code>@InjectMocks</code> annotation with mockito, then yes, the framework would take care of the injection of the mock into the class under test (regardless of the existence of the setters etc.).</p>

<p>Another thing is the <code>playerMock</code>, which i assume is the class under test.</p>

<p>Do not mock the class under test, create a normal instance and then inject the dependencies... the <code>performWaiting</code> method does not seem to accept the response String, so you would have to inject that also somehow (unless you left some parts out):</p>

<pre><code>@Test
public void test() {
    // Arrange
    String jsonResponse = "{\"status\":\"waiting\",\"no\":\"12\"}";     
    Gson gsonMock = PowerMockito.mock(Gson.class);
    ExtraPlayer extraPlayer = new ExtraPlayer();
    extraPlayer.setNo("12");
    extraPlayer.setStatus("Waiting");

    PowerMockito.when(gsonMock.fromJson(jsonResponse, ExtraPlayer.class)).thenReturn(extraPlayer);

    Player player = new Player();
    player.setGson(gsonMock);
    player.setResponse(jsonResponse);

    // Act
    player.performWaiting();

    // Assert ...
}
</code></pre>
